What's Zylpheed
===============

Zylpheed is a 64-bit port of the Sylpheed email client based on GTK3 GUI toolkit.
It is developed and tested on 64-bit Microsoft Windows 10 and 11.

Zylpheed is an independent fork. It is not produced or supported by the
Sylpheed project, so please do not report Zylpheed problems there
(see "Information" below).

Zylpheed is a free software distributed under the GNU GPL (the library part
LibSylph is GNU LGPL).

Current status
--------------

    * The port is new and has had little real-world testing.
    * No security review of this fork has been done yet. Treat it as
      pre-release software.
    * Platforms: 64-bit Windows 10 and 11 are the only platforms built and
      tested so far. Linux, BSD and macOS are planned targets; they are not
      yet built or tested with this port.
    * 32-bit Windows and Windows 7/8 are not supported.

Differences from Sylpheed
-------------------------

    * GTK+ 3.24 instead of GTK+ 2. GTK2 themes and gtkrc files are not
      used. An optional gtk.css in the configuration directory can be used
      to adjust the appearance.
    * Zylpheed keeps its own configuration directory, so it can be installed
      beside Sylpheed. It does not read or migrate a Sylpheed profile.
      The files inside the directory keep Sylpheed's names and formats
      (sylpheedrc, accountrc, folderlist.xml, ...).
    * Plug-ins built for Sylpheed (GTK2) cannot be loaded. They have to be
      ported and rebuilt; see PLUGIN.txt.
    * The update check is disabled.
    * Spell checking uses GtkSpell 3 with Enchant 2.
    * OpenPGP needs a separately installed GnuPG (for example Gpg4win),
      as with Sylpheed.

Zylpheed has the following features:

    * Simple, beautiful, and well-polished user interface
    * Comfortable operationality which is built in detail
    * Well-organized, easy-to-understand configuration
    * Lightweight operation
    * High reliability
    * Internationalization and Multilingualization support
    * High-level Japanese processing
    * Various protocols support
    * Multi-thread support
    * Security feature (GnuPG, SSL/TLS)
    * Powerful filtering and search
    * Junk mail control
    * Flexible cooperation with external commands
    * Plug-in faculty

The appearance and interface are similar to some popular e-mail clients for
Windows, such as Outlook or Becky!. Many commands are accessible with
the keyboard like the Emacs-based mailers such as Mew and Wanderlust.
So you will be able to migrate to Zylpheed without much discomfort, even if
you are accustomed to other mailers.

The messages are managed by MH format, and you will be able to use it together
with another mailer based on MH format (like Mew). It has less possibility
of losing mails on failures since one file corresponds to one mail. You can
import or export mbox and eml format messages. You can also utilize fetchmail
and/or procmail, and external programs on receiving (like inc or imget).

Currently implemented major features
====================================

Supported protocols

	o POP3
	o IMAP4rev1
	o SMTP
	o NNTP
	o SSL/TLS (POP3, SMTP, IMAP4rev1, NNTP)
	o IPv6
	o SOCKS4/5

Features

	o multiple accounts
	o powerful filtering (POP3, IMAP4, local spool)
	o powerful search (query search, quick search, search folder)
	o Junk mail control
	o plug-in faculty
	o thread display
	o display and transfer of attachments by MIME
	o inline image display
	o News reader
	o SMTP AUTH support (PLAIN / LOGIN / CRAM-MD5 / OAuth2)
	o CRAM-MD5 authentication (SMTP AUTH / IMAP4rev1)
	o APOP authentication (POP3)
	o OAuth2 authentication (POP3 / IMAP4 / SMTP)
	o PGP signature / encryption (requires GPGME and GnuPG)
	o spell checking (requires GtkSpell 3)
	o SOCKS4/5 proxy support
	o X-Face display
	o user-defined headers
	o mark / color label
	o Mew/Wanderlust compatible key bind
	o multiple MH folder support
	o mbox and eml import / export
	o action function to work with external commands
	  (not available on Windows)
	o external editor
	o message queueing
	o automatic mail checking
	o draft message
	o template
	o line-wrapping
	o autosave
	o clickable URI
	o address book
	o newly arrived and unread message management
	o printing
	o POP3 remote mailbox
	o offline mode
	o tray icon (minimize to tray icon, new mail notification)
	o customization of toolbar
	o remote operation by command line
	o per-folder configuration
	o LDAP, vCard, and JPilot support in address book
	  (LDAP and JPilot are not built on Windows)
	o CSV, LDIF file import in address book
	o auto-registration of recipient's addresses to address book
	o auto-completion of addresses
	o Drag & Drop
	o autoconf, automake support
	o internationalization of messages by gettext
	o m17n (multilingualization) support

and more.

Installation
============

For 64-bit Windows, see BUILD-WIN64.md. It describes the MSYS2 build
environment, the build itself, and how to make the portable folder and
the installer.

INSTALL is the original Sylpheed document. It describes the GTK2 build
and has not been updated for Zylpheed.

Usage
=====

Preparation before running (Unix-like systems)
----------------------------------------------

If you are using a character encoding other than UTF-8 for filename,
you must set the following environmental variable (it will not work if
it is not specified):

(use locale-specific encoding)
% export G_FILENAME_ENCODING=@locale

or

(manually specifying encoding)
% export G_FILENAME_ENCODING=ISO-8859-1

If you want to display messages translated to your language,
you must specify some environmental variables related to locale.
For example:

% export LANG=de_DE.UTF-8

(replace de_DE.UTF-8 to the actual locale name)

If you don't want translated messages, set LC_MESSAGES to "C"
(and unset LC_ALL if specified).

How to run
----------

Input `zylpheed' on a command line, or double-click the icon in a file
manager to execute.

Initial startup
---------------

When Zylpheed is executed for the first time, it automatically creates the
configuration files and asks you the location of mailbox. If some files,
which are non-MH format, already exist on the directory, you will have to
specify another location.

The configuration directory is:

	Windows			%APPDATA%\Zylpheed
	Mac OS X		~/Library/Application Support/Zylpheed
	other Unix-like systems	~/.zylpheed

A different directory can be given with the --configdir command line
option.

Zylpheed does not use the configuration directory of Sylpheed
(%APPDATA%\Sylpheed or ~/.sylpheed-2.0) and does not migrate it. If you
want to reuse Sylpheed settings, copy the contents of that directory into
the Zylpheed one while neither program is running. Be aware that the copy
includes the account settings and any stored passwords.

On Unix-like systems, if the locale encoding is not UTF-8 and the
environmental variable G_FILENAME_ENCODING is not set, an alert dialog
will be displayed.

Required settings
-----------------

Initially, you have to create at least one account to send or receive
messages (you can read messages that already exist without creating
accounts). The configuration dialog will be shown by clicking
"Configuration -> Create new account..." in the menu, or "Account" on
the toolbar. Then, fill the required settings.

The manual supplied with this program is the Sylpheed manual. It describes
the general usage, and still refers to the program as Sylpheed.

Hidden settings
---------------

You can configure most of the features in Zylpheed through the preferences
dialog, but there are a few parameters which don't have user interface
(you don't need to modify them in normal use). You must edit the file
sylpheedrc in the configuration directory by an editor while Zylpheed is
not running. (The file keeps the name used by Sylpheed.)

allow_jisx0201_kana		allow JIS X 0201 Kana (half-width kana) on
				sending
				0: off  1: on   [default: 0]
translate_header		translate headers such as 'From:', 'To:' and
				'Subject:'
				0: off  1: on   [default: 1]
enable_rules_hint		enable alternating row colors for summary view
				0: off  1: on   [default: 1]
bold_unread			show unread messages using bold font in
				summary view
				0: off  1: on   [default: 1]
textview_cursor_visible		show cursor in the text view
				0: off  1: on   [default: 0]
mime_image_viewer		specify the command to open attached image
				files [default: none]
mime_audio_player		specify the command to open attached audio
				files [default: none]
mime_command			specify the command to open MIME attachments
				[default: none]
logwindow_line_limit		specify the limit of line numbers of log
				window
				0: unlimited  n (> 0): n lines [default: 1000]
mark_as_read_on_new_window	only mark messages as read when they are
				opened with new window
				0: off  1: on   [default: 0]

Menu shortcuts cannot be changed directly in the menus (GTK3 no longer
supports that). You can use the following methods to configure them:

1. Choose one of the key binding presets in the common preferences.

2. When Zylpheed is not running, directly edit the file menurc in the
   configuration directory with an editor.

Information
===========

The source code and the issue tracker of Zylpheed are at:

	https://github.com/creathcarter/zylpheed

Please report bugs in Zylpheed there, not to the Sylpheed project.

Zylpheed is based on Sylpheed 3.8.0beta1. Information about Sylpheed,
the original program, is at:

	https://sylpheed.sraoss.jp/

The Sylpheed documentation in English and several other languages can be
obtained from:

	http://sylpheeddoc.sourceforge.net/

It was written for Sylpheed, and most of it applies to Zylpheed as well.

README.es and README.ja are the original Sylpheed documents and have not
been updated for Zylpheed.

Feedback
========

Comments, ideas and (most of all) bug reports (and especially patches) are
very welcome at the GitHub address above.

Source code
===========

You can get the newest source code from the Git repository:

	git clone https://github.com/creathcarter/zylpheed.git

Credits
=======

Sylpheed was written by Hiroyuki Yamamoto <hiro-y@kcn.ne.jp> and
contributors; see AUTHORS.

Ported to GTK3 and 64-bit Windows by Creath and Claude.
